# Test Finite-Horizon Control Reachability

> This is an authored finite mathematical or logical case supported by bounded source reading. Human technical review, fluent-language review, learner attempts, native runtime execution, production adoption and publication decisions remain undecided.

An agent coordinator can observe a queue state without having actions that can move every component of that state. Adding more telemetry does not create missing actuators. Before proposing a feedback DSL, describe which state variables each allowed action can change. This lesson uses a stipulated linear recurrence to separate controllability from the observability studied earlier.

Expand the recurrence over the declared horizon from the stated initial condition. Earlier inputs are multiplied by the dynamics before the final observation; later inputs have fewer transitions to traverse. Put those contributions into columns in input-time order. Their span determines the reachable final states when controls are unrestricted real values. Rank alone describes the dimension of that span, while solving the target equation checks the particular requested endpoint.

The fixed actuator can influence both state coordinates over the horizon, although it directly touches only one coordinate. The control changes the actuator direction and leaves a coordinate unreachable. Repeated calls cannot manufacture the missing direction. This does not model saturation, queue discreteness, human authority or delay. A reachable algebraic target is therefore a narrow feasibility statement, not permission to execute actions or a demonstration of stable closed-loop operation.

